{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788135233,"nonce":"BetGukIITPpM43Tjk3coql+3aEY=","message":"WORKER REPORT — opus worker, orchestrator session 5, BORGES (Windows).\nBranch `spec/2.2-pawn-serializers`, isolated worktree. Four NEW files, no\nexisting file touched, no DLL committed. `dotnet build -c Release` clean,\n0 warnings.\n\n    6583808  Pawn read-safety layer: the accessors whose READ mutates the pawn\n    c85189d  pawns + pawn: the roster glance and the per-pawn drill-down\n    ee9b2df  pawn-fixture: the acceptance stimulus the bench cannot otherwise produce\n\nFiles: `Source/AutoRimmer/PawnSafe.cs` (hazard catalogue + guarded routes),\n`PawnSerializer.cs` (section builders), `PawnVerbs.cs` (`pawns`, `pawn`),\n`PawnFixtureVerbs.cs` (`pawn-fixture`, dev-gated).\n\n\n\n`needs[].arrow` and `mood.arrow` are the needs tab's OWN trajectory arrow\n(+1 rising / 0 steady / -1 falling), with `instant_pct` beside it — the\ninstant marker the tab draws on the bar (`CurInstantLevelPercentage`, null\nwhere the need has no instant level, since `Need.CurInstantLevel` is -1 by\ndefault and only the seeker needs override it).\n\nRationale. A sampled trend needs a per-pawn ring of past values, and that\nring has to live somewhere: a GameComponent (scribed, so an observer would\nbe adding fields to the SAVE) or a static dictionary (which then goes stale\nacross load, wrong after `advance`, and unbounded as pawns come and go).\nEither way the observer holds mutable state, which is the thing this spec's\ninvariant forbids. A journal-derived trend is worse still: nothing is\nemitted when a continuous value moves, which is exactly the gap DESIGN's\nown decisions-log entry records for `advance until:condition`.\n\nAnd the arrow is not a compromise — it is the number the player reads.\n2.1 already publishes `mood_arrow` from the same accessor, so this is one\nconvention rather than two.\n\nVerified: `RimWorld/Need.cs` `CurLevel` is a plain `curLevelInt` read;\n`GUIChangeArrow` is virtual, defaulting to 0, overridden per need\n(`Need_Seeker` compares instant vs current with a 0.05 tolerance,\n`Need_Rest` is `Resting ? 1 : -1`, `Need_Joy` keys on a gain tick). None\nwrites. `Need_Mood.CurInstantLevel` memoizes `lastInstantMood` per tick\ninto a non-scribed private field.\n\n\n`mood.thoughts` is `ThoughtHandler.GetDistinctMoodThoughtGroups` filtered\nby `VisibleInNeedsTab`, with each group's offset from\n`MoodOffsetOfGroup` — i.e. what `PawnNeedsUIUtility\n.GetThoughtGroupsInDisplayOrder` builds. Two deliberate differences from\nthat method:\n\n- It writes `Thought.cachedMoodOffsetOfGroup` on every group as its sort\n  scratch. We do not. Observers do not write, and there is no need: the\n  offset goes straight into our own key.\n- It ties on `GetHashCode()`. We tie on the group's `defName`. A hash is\n  not stable run to run — fine for a UI list, fatal for an rwtest\n  assertion on which thought got truncated.\n\nDisplay: sorted by offset DESCENDING, then the HEAD and the TAIL of that\norder are emitted (up to 6 each way), which is what \"top ± thoughts\" means.\nThe middle is what gets dropped and the middle is the near-zero material —\nthe right thing to lose. `thoughts_more` publishes the count. Each line\ncarries `offset` (the GROUP's, from the game's stacking maths — not\n`baseMoodEffect × count`, which disagrees with the game whenever\n`stackedEffectMultiplier != 1`) and `count`.\n\nOne trap worth recording: `MoodOffsetOfGroup` fills a private STATIC\n`tmpThoughts` inside `ThoughtHandler`. Passing the handler's own scratch\nlist to `GetDistinctMoodThoughtGroups` and then iterating it while calling\n`MoodOffsetOfGroup` would clobber the list mid-iteration. We pass our own\n`List\u003cThought\u003e`.\n\n\nDefault (`relations.scope == \"direct-only\"`): the `DirectRelations` backing\nlist, filtered to nothing, sorted on-map-first then by `def.importance`,\ncapped at 16. Each line is `{id, name, relation, label, class, on_map,\ndead}`. Zero writes, zero graph walks — that covers spouse / lover / parent\n/ child / sibling and animal bonds, which is what \"colony-relevant\" means\nin practice.\n\n`opinions:true` (cap 12, max 12) adds `social[]`, `best` and `worst`\n— the \"rivals\" half of the spec's ask, without making the caller scan.\nIt is OFF by default and the result carries the disclosure\n(`relations.opinions = {enabled, cap, queried, pool, skipped, note}`).\n\nRationale, and this is the interesting one. `OpinionOf` is NOT a read —\nconfirmed in `RimWorld/SituationalThoughtHandler.cs`: `AppendSocialThoughts`\ncalls `CheckRecalculateSocialThoughts`, which CREATES a\n`CachedSocialThoughts` entry for the (observer, other) pair when none\nexists, instantiates `Thought_SituationalSocial` objects, and stamps\n`lastQueryTick`, which `Expired` keys on — so querying also keeps entries\nalive that would otherwise be reaped. Reaping runs in\n`SituationalThoughtInterval`, which does not run while PAUSED, and this\nbench is paused by default. On top of that each call runs `GetRelations`,\nwhose Kin worker walks `FamilyByBlood_Internal` — a full family-graph BFS.\nN pawns is N BFS walks.\n\nBut: that cache is NOT scribed. `ThoughtHandler.ExposeData` scribes only\n`memories`; `SituationalThoughtHandler` has no `ExposeData` at all. So this\nis a cost-and-allocation hazard, not a save-corrupting one — categorically\ndifferent from the policy trackers, which write a `Scribe_References` field.\nThat difference is why it is offered at all rather than banned, and why it\nis off rather than on.\n\nThe query set is DETERMINISTIC — spawned, visible, humanlike colony members\nother than self, ordered by `thingIDNumber` — so repeated calls perturb the\nSAME pairs. A per-call cap alone would let a loop of calls touch every pair\nin the colony; ordering bounds the total across a session.\n\nNot used, and why: `PawnRelationUtility.GetMostImportantRelation` is\nwrite-free and is the right accessor for \"what is X to Y\", but it is still\none `GetRelations` walk per pair — and `DirectRelations` answers the same\nquestion for free wherever there IS a direct relation. `SocialCardUtility`\nis out except `GetPawnSituationLabel`, and that one runs\n`GetAllQuestPartsOfType\u003cQuestPart_LendColonistsToFaction\u003e()` with a LINQ\nscan per call. `GetMostImportantColonyRelative` runs `GetRelations` against\nevery free colonist and prisoner on every map.\n\n\nConfirmed in `RimWorld/StatWorker.cs`: `GetValue(Thing, ...)` writes\n`immutableStatCache` (a `ConcurrentDictionary`) or `temporaryStatCache` (a\nplain `Dictionary`, present whenever `stat.cacheable`) on EVERY call, and\nthe write is not gated on the `cacheStaleAfterTicks` argument. There is no\nread-only mode. `Thing.MaxHitPoints` IS a `GetStatValue` call\n(`Verse/Thing.cs`), so apparel HP% cannot avoid it, and neither can the\nmental-break thresholds (`MentalBreaker` is three `GetStatValue` calls on\n`MentalBreakThreshold`) or `Need_Food.MaxLevel`.\n\nResolution: **use it, and be precise about what \"observers never mutate\"\nprotects.** What that invariant is about is GAME STATE — anything scribed,\nanything the simulation can observe, anything that makes a rerun differ.\n`temporaryStatCache` is none of those: it is a memo keyed by `Thing`, not\nsaved, recomputed from the same inputs, and the identical write the vanilla\ninspect pane performs every frame the cursor rests on an item. Refusing it\nwould mean refusing to report apparel HP% at all — and the clothes\nchecklist, which is the whole reason apparel is in this spec, is built on\nexactly that number.\n\nTwo things follow that are not optional:\n\n- **Confinement.** Stat calls live in the `pawn \u003cid\u003e` drill-down only. The\n  `pawns` hot path makes NO stat call. So the cost lands on a verb called\n  once per pawn on demand, never on a verb called constantly.\n- **The main-thread rule is now a correctness requirement, not tidiness.**\n  `temporaryStatCache` is a plain `Dictionary`, not a concurrent one.\n  `GetStatValue` off the main thread corrupts it. This is an independent\n  proof of the project's threading discipline and it is written into\n  `PawnSafe`'s header so the next reader finds it.\n\n\nIt held up well. Everything load-bearing checked out. Three corrections:\n\n1. **`curPolicy` is wrong for the outfit tracker in 1.6.** The field is\n   `Pawn_OutfitTracker.curApparelPolicy` (scribed as `\"curOutfit\"`). The\n   food and drug trackers really are `curPolicy`. An `AccessTools` ref built\n   from the research's name would have silently failed to bind — which is\n   why `PawnSafe.Policies` publishes a `source` field rather than reporting\n   a failed ref as \"no policy\".\n\n2. **`RelatedPawns` is less dangerous than stated.** It does set\n   `canCacheFamilyByBlood = true` and clear it only in the iterator's\n   `finally` — but a `foreach` that `break`s still disposes the enumerator\n   and still runs the `finally`, as do `.Any()` and `.First()`. The leak\n   needs a hand-obtained enumerator that is never disposed. Not used here\n   either way.\n\n3. **`GuestStatus` is `{Guest, Prisoner, Slave}`** — confirmed, and\n   `Pawn.GuestStatus` really does return null for a plain visitor. The\n   classification ladder in `PawnSafe.Classify` derives eleven classes from\n   the tightest-first tests, and every one of them is a legal `filter` word.\n\nNewly found, not in the research: `Pawn_AgeTracker.CurLifeStageIndex`'s\nrecalc reaches `pawn.Drawer.renderer.SetAllGraphicsDirty()`,\n`CheckChangePawnKindName()` — which can rename the pawn — and\n`PawnComponentsUtility.AddAndRemoveDynamicComponents(pawn)`. The research\nflagged this class; the confirmed consequence is worse than \"rebuilds\ntrackers\". Nothing here reads it: ages come from `AgeBiologicalYears`.\n\nAlso confirmed independently against `RimWorld/HealthCardUtility.cs`: the\npercentage the health tab PRINTS for a capacity is\n`PawnCapacityUtility.CalculateCapacityLevel`, and the cached\n`capacities.GetLevel` is used only to choose the label COLOUR. Matching the\ntab means calling the pure function.\n\n\n`pawn-fixture` — a NEW dev verb in its own file, gated on `Prefs.DevMode`,\njournalling a `dev` event per step, superseded by 3.1. Steps:\n`wound | sadden | tatter | prisoner | visitor`.\n\nIt exists because journal-selftest belongs to the parallel 1.5/1.7 worker\nthis wave and could not be extended, and because four of the five states\n2.2's acceptance names cannot be produced by any shipped verb:\n`journal-selftest downed` gives a DOWNED pawn (which hides half the surface\nunder test) rather than a wounded standing one, and **a prisoner has no\nroute at all before 3.4's capture verbs / 3.1's dev spawn** — so `prisoner`\ngenerates a non-hostile outlander and hands it to the game's own capture\npath, `Pawn_GuestTracker.SetGuestStatus`.\n\nEvery step returns its own hand-computed expectation beside what it did\n(injuries read back off the hediff set, group offsets from the game's own\nmaths, expected wear labels, expected class/resistance/will), so acceptance\ncompares two readers rather than letting the serializer grade itself. That\nis the `stockpile` step's discipline.\n\n\nIts `dev` row says \"3.1 owns the type; `journal-selftest` writes it today\".\nThere is now a second writer, `pawn-fixture`. I did not touch the file —\nit belongs to the parallel worker.\n\n\nNo resolution changes another spec's contract. `id` is `thingIDNumber`,\nwhich is already what `Positions.Resolve`'s `pawn:\u003cid\u003e` takes, so the two\nsurfaces agree.","files":null}]}